Although I don't fully follow what it is you're trying to get at here, it's
generally agreed upon that using ka that way is no good. In general use, ka
is used only for "pure functions" -- properties and not qualitites, which
is how the CLL describes ka.

That being said, I'd just use {nu}, which I believe represents an
particular instance of a predication, which is just a property with the
ce'u-places filled in:

{lo nu lo fagri cu glare cu pluka mi}
or perhaps,
{lo nu seli'i lo fagri cu glare cu pluka mi}

We can say it more poetically with
{.i mi selpu'a co seli'i fagri glare}

.i mi'e la tsani mu'o

